event: response.function_call_arguments.done
data: {"type":"response.function_call_arguments.done","sequence_number":992,"item_id":"fc_04de6b92baab60ea016adb464ab7048191b3f34c1016d7b555","output_index":1,"arguments":"{\"artifact\":{\"base_candidate\":34,\"patches\":[{\"op\":\"add\",\"path\":\"/graph/propositions/-\",\"value\":{\"id\":\"M018\",\"kind\":\"mathlib\",\"statement\":\"In a Hausdorff topological space, every compact set is closed.\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"X\",\"type\":\"topological space\"},{\"symbol\":\"K\",\"type\":\"subset of X\"}],\"objects\":[\"X\",\"K\"],\"source\":{\"declaration\":\"IsCompact.isClosed\",\"module\":\"Mathlib.Topology.Separation.Hausdorff\",\"hypotheses\":[\"X is a topological space.\",\"X is Hausdorff.\",\"K is compact.\"],\"evidence\":[{\"path\":\"library/M018-0019/library-sources.json\",\"sha256\":\"388d0738599bce28a7c9ac059f858531d8bc830ecb41a602c6e87d137215847b\"}]}}},{\"op\":\"replace\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/1/inputs\",\"value\":[\"H003\",\"M001\",\"M016\",\"M013\",\"M014\",\"M018\"]},{\"op\":\"replace\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/1/argument/0/uses\",\"value\":[\"H003\",\"M016\",\"M018\"]},{\"op\":\"replace\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/1/argument/1/uses\",\"value\":[\"M001\",\"M013\",\"M014\",\"M018\"]},{\"op\":\"replace\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/1/argument/1/text\",\"value\":\"Choose R₀ using F001 and define Q=∏_{i=1}^n[-R₀,R₀]. Each coordinate has absolute value at most ‖x‖, hence K⊆Q. M013 and M014 give μ(Q)=∏_{i=1}^n μ([-R₀,R₀])=(2R₀)^n<∞. Apply M018 to the compact K in the Hausdorff Euclidean space to conclude K is closed and hence Borel measurable; monotonicity yields μ(K)≤μ(Q)<∞. As K was arbitrary, Lebesgue measure is finite on compact sets. The Borel measurable structure on ℝⁿ is the σ-algebra generated by Euclidean opens, so opens are measurable. Joint smoothness and u=q on the open spatial complement of C throughout J imply all first spatial and time derivatives, and the second and mixed derivatives entering e_t, vanish outside C. Thus e(·,t) is continuous with compact support in C. Apply M001 to conclude e(·,t) is integrable. The same smoothness/support argument shows u_t(·,s), u_{x_j}(·,s), e(·,s), and e_t(·,s) are continuous and vanish off C for each s∈J and j=1,…,n. This proves the claimed output for arbitrary t>0.\"},{\"op\":\"replace\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/1/applications\",\"value\":[{\"input\":\"M001\",\"at\":\"S002\",\"bindings\":\"X=ℝⁿ with its Euclidean topology and Borel measurable space, V=ℝ, μ=Lebesgue measure, f=e(·,t).\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"ℝⁿ has a measurable space and Euclidean topology.\",\"Its Borel measurable space makes Euclidean opens measurable.\",\"ℝ is a normed additive commutative group.\",\"S001–S002 prove Lebesgue measure is finite on every compact subset of ℝⁿ.\",\"e(·,t) is continuous and has compact support in C by smoothness and H003.\"]},{\"input\":\"M016\",\"at\":\"S001\",\"bindings\":\"X=ℝⁿ with its Euclidean pseudometric; K is an arbitrary compact subset fixed in S001.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"ℝⁿ carries the Euclidean pseudometric.\",\"K is compact by the local arbitrary declaration in S001.\"]},{\"input\":\"M013\",\"at\":\"S002\",\"bindings\":\"ι=Fin n, α(i)=ℝ, s(i)=[−R₀,R₀] for each i.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"Fin n is finite because n is a positive integer.\",\"Each coordinate ℝ is a measure space and its volume measure is sigma-finite.\"]},{\"input\":\"M014\",\"at\":\"S002\",\"bindings\":\"a=−R₀ and b=R₀.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"a and b are real numbers; the source theorem has no additional hypotheses.\"]},{\"input\":\"M018\",\"at\":\"S002\",\"bindings\":\"X=ℝⁿ with Euclidean topology and Hausdorff separation; K is the arbitrary compact set introduced in S001.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"X carries the Euclidean topology.\",\"Euclidean space is Hausdorff.\",\"K is compact by its arbitrary local declaration.\"]}]}]}}"}

